<BaseLayout title="Where the Fence Meets the Sea — Huanyu Chen"
  description="A Día de Muertos weekend in Tijuana: the painted border fence at Playas, Caesar's and Avenida Revolución, and the long line home through San Ysidro.">
  <p class="muted"><a href="/misc">← Misc</a> · October 2022</p>
  <h1>Where the Fence Meets the Sea</h1>
  <p class="muted trip-sub">A weekend in Tijuana, a few days before the Day of the Dead.</p>

  <div class="callout trip-stats">
    <strong>3 days · October 28–30, 2022</strong><br />
    Los Angeles → San Diego → across the border on Friday night → a night in Tijuana → Playas de Tijuana → Avenida Revolución → a second night in Tijuana → the San Ysidro line →
    home<br />
    two nights in Tijuana · one border fence painted end to end · two giant Catrinas and a skeleton wedding · one salad cart with a hundred years of
    history · hours in the line to go home
  </div>

  <p>
    Tijuana is close. From most of Southern California it is a shorter drive than Las Vegas or San Francisco, and
    the border sits about twenty miles south of downtown San Diego. Plenty of people cross it every day without
    thinking much about it. For the two of us it had stayed a name on the freeway signs, the last exit before Mexico,
    until a weekend at the end of October 2022. Día de Muertos was a few days away, and the city was getting
    ready for it.
  </p>
  <p>
    That was the whole reason for going. The Day of the Dead is one of the great festivals of the year, and the
    celebrations everyone pictures, the marigold-strewn cemeteries and the parades of painted skulls, belong to Mexico
    City and the south. Those are a long flight away. Tijuana is a morning's drive. If we wanted to see a Mexican city
    dressing up for its dead, this was the one within reach.
  </p>
  <p>
    As with the other trips on this page, everything below is reconstructed from the camera roll: when each photo was
    taken, and where. The record is good for Saturday and patchy for Sunday, and where it is silent I've said so rather
    than guess. The weekend has a simple shape. Saturday morning was the ocean end of the border, where the fence runs
    down the beach and into the surf. Saturday afternoon was downtown, on Avenida Revolución, which was halfway through
    dressing up for the Day of the Dead. Sunday was brunch, a last look at downtown and the long, slow line north.
  </p>

  <h2>Day 1: The fence and the sea</h2>
  <p>
    Tijuana is a border city, so there was no flight and no plan beyond the car: we drove down from Los Angeles
    on Friday evening, past San Diego, and crossed the border after dark to spend the night in Tijuana, so Saturday began in the city. By eleven
    on Saturday morning we were in <strong>Playas de Tijuana</strong>, the beach neighborhood in the far
    northwest corner of the city, and of Mexico. The day was clear, with a cold, deep blue sky and the kind of hard,
    low October sun that makes every color look freshly painted. Playas sits on a bluff above a long sand beach. A
    wide malecón, the seafront promenade, runs along the top, lined with palms, seafood places and little plazas. At
    its north end the city stops, abruptly, at a wall of rusting steel posts.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-1029.webp'}><img src={P + 'daymap-1029.webp'} alt="Map of the route: Day 1: Playas de Tijuana in the morning, then downtown and Avenida Revolución in the afternoon." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Day 1: Playas de Tijuana in the morning, then downtown for the afternoon.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h3>Here begins the homeland</h3>
  <p>
    The first thing you see on the promenade is the town's name, in letters taller than a person, each one a different
    color and covered in little painted tiles: red, orange, green, blue, yellow, and a big red A with white seagulls
    flying across it. A pair of leaping dolphins rides on top, and the palms lean overhead. Along the base runs
    Tijuana's motto, <em>Aquí empieza la Patria</em>, "Here begins the homeland". It is a play on geography: this is the
    northwestern tip of the country, so for Mexico, this is where the map starts. Signs like this have become a
    fixture in Mexican towns, and like all of them, this one is mostly a backdrop for photos. We spent a quarter of an
    hour there doing exactly that, and none of those photos are here.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'playas-tijuana-sign.webp'} alt="Giant multicolored letters spelling TIJUANA on the Playas promenade, with palm trees, a dolphin sculpture on top and the motto Aquí empieza la Patria along the base." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>"Aquí empieza la Patria": the Tijuana sign on the Playas promenade.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>Into the surf</h3>
  <p>
    A few steps north, the promenade ends at the border, and the border keeps going. The fence here is a row of tall
    steel posts set a hand's width apart. It runs down the beach, across the sand and straight out into the Pacific,
    past the line where the waves break, before it finally stops in open water. The posts at the waterline are
    streaked with rust and salt, orange and black and white, and the surf piles up against them and pours through the
    gaps. It is a strange thing to look at. Fences are supposed to end somewhere, and this one simply walks into the
    sea. From the top of the bluff you can see all of it at once: the painted fence running down across the sand
    and straight into the surf.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig"><img src={P + 'fence-from-bluff.webp'} alt="The painted border fence seen from the bluff, running across the beach and out into the ocean under a deep blue sky, with small figures on the sand and canopies in the foreground." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The whole fence from the bluff, 10:51 am.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The seabirds have their own opinion of it. That morning a row of cormorants stood along the top rail at the
    seaward end, with a couple of gulls keeping their distance a few posts along.
    To them it is just a convenient perch with an ocean view. Behind the fence, on the other side, the beach carries
    on exactly as before, the same sand, the same surf, for miles toward Imperial Beach. Every few seconds a wave
    came in, ran up between the posts and slid back out, the only thing in sight crossing freely in both
    directions.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'fence-into-pacific.webp'} alt="The rusted steel border fence running out into the Pacific Ocean, with seabirds perched on the top rail." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The fence walks into the Pacific.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'fence-seabirds.webp'} alt="Cormorants and gulls perched along the top of the border fence above the waves." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Cormorants on the top rail.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    This corner has a longer history than the steel suggests. In 1971 the area around the border monument here was
    dedicated as <strong>Friendship Park</strong>, a place where people from both countries could meet, and for decades
    families divided by the border came to see and talk to each other through the fence. The
    barrier has been rebuilt and made taller several times since, and the meeting area on the U.S. side has been
    closed for long stretches. On the Mexican side, though, the fence is right there at the end of the promenade,
    open to anyone who walks up to it, and over the years people have turned it into something else entirely.
  </p>

  <h3>A gallery on steel posts</h3>
  <p>
    Starting at the beach, the fence is painted almost all the way along. Near the water, the first stretch is
    a row of enormous faces, men and women in black and white and blue-gray, each several posts wide and as tall
    as the fence. Because the steel posts are set apart, every face is sliced into vertical strips with sky showing
    between them. Up close it falls apart into stripes; from twenty steps back, it snaps into a portrait. Stand
    further back still and the row of faces marches down the beach toward the waves, under a coil of razor wire
    along the top. A large QR code had been fixed to one post among them, linking the artwork to a mural project.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'murals-faces.webp'} alt="A row of large black-and-white portrait faces painted across the vertical posts of the border fence, under a deep blue sky with razor wire on top." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Portraits painted across the posts.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'murals-beach.webp'} alt="The painted border fence running down the beach toward the surf, with portrait murals along its length." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The same row of faces, heading for the waves.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Walking inland along the fence, the style changes every few yards, as if a hundred different people had each
    been given one section, which is more or less what happened. Above the beach, a run of posts is painted in reds,
    pinks and yellows, covered in hearts and handwritten names, with one word stenciled across the tops in black
    capitals: <em>CORAZÓN</em>, "heart". Between the posts you can see straight through to the other side: a strip of
    sand, a second fence, the Tijuana River valley and the hills of San Diego beyond.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'corazon.webp'} alt="The tops of brightly painted fence posts with the word CORAZÓN spelled out across them in black letters against blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption><em>Corazón</em>, spelled across the posts.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Some of the art is professional, and some is a piece of cardboard. Wired to the steel mesh at one point was a
    hand-lettered sign: <em>Let us see our families</em>, with a broken heart drawn in place of the O, and below it,
    <em>No walls, just hugs</em>, signed off with four little hearts. It was faded and a little warped by the weather. A few posts later came a large painted doorway, the
    <em>Mural de la Hermandad</em> ("mural of brotherhood"), signed by the Tijuana artist Enrique Chiu. A red heart with
    LOVE across it fills the bars, framed by the words <em>Un mundo sin muros</em>, "a world without walls", and
    flanked by narrow posts painted with names, many of them followed by a branch of the U.S. military.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'let-us-see.webp'} alt="A weathered cardboard sign wired to the fence reading Let Us See Our Families, No Walls Just Hugs." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>"Let us see our families."</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'love-heart.webp'} alt="A blue painted section of fence with a big red heart reading LOVE, framed by the words Un mundo sin muros." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>"Un mundo sin muros."</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Pull back and the scale of it becomes clear. The LOVE doorway is one panel among dozens: to its left, a long
    run of pale posts each carries a name in a different hand; to its right come seafoam-green posts lettered with
    words like <em>esperanza</em> and <em>felicidad</em>, hope and happiness. A little further on, a stretch of fence
    is painted as an American flag, the stars and stripes running down the posts and fading where the paint has
    weathered, with a small vegetable garden growing in a frame of white pipe at its foot.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'mural-hermandad.webp'} alt="A long view of the painted border fence: a blue doorway with a red LOVE heart, posts covered in handwritten names, and green posts beyond." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The <em>Mural de la Hermandad</em>, and the names beside it.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'flag-fence.webp'} alt="Fence posts painted as a weathered American flag, with a small garden in a white pipe frame in front." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Stars and stripes on the posts, a garden at their feet.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The biggest single mural was the last before the plaza. Along a long section of fence beside the sidewalk, a
    giant brown hand reaches out of a field of bright greens and yellows, palm open as if releasing something, with
    an avocado and two faces painted in the panels along the top. The green runs for a good fifty yards. From the
    end of the sidewalk, the fence and its mural recede in a long diagonal toward the palms and the ocean, and you
    can see a long way down the line of paint.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'hand-mural.webp'} alt="A long mural on the border fence beside a sidewalk: a giant hand reaching out of a field of green and yellow, with an avocado painted above." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The hand, fifty yards of green.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>Todos somos hermanos</h3>
  <p>
    The fence along this stretch doubles as the back wall of a small park. Tucked under a leaning tree, in front of
    posts painted green, red, yellow and navy, is a concrete bench that someone painted dark blue and filled with
    stars, handprints and a ringed planet. Across its back it says <em>Somos la misma raza</em>, "we are the same
    people", and across the seat, in bubbly green letters with a heart, <em>Todos somos hermanos</em>, "we are all
    brothers and sisters". It is a good bench, in the sun, out of the wind, and it got sat on for a while, which is all a
    bench really asks.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'todos-somos-hermanos.webp'} alt="A concrete bench painted with stars and handprints, reading Somos la misma raza and Todos somos hermanos, in front of a multicolored fence." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The bench: "we are the same people", "we are all brothers and sisters".</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    On the lawn by the plaza, a set of giant hearts stands on steel posts, each one painted by a different artist.
    One is a desert sunset in red, orange and yellow, with small figures walking across it. Another is an eagle,
    the two lobes of the heart turned into a pair of folded brown wings with a white head tucked between them, so
    from one side it is a heart and from the other it is a bird. Behind them, the fence carries on, painted with
    a portrait of two faces leaning together, and past that the low hills of the other side.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'heart-sculpture.webp'} alt="A large painted heart sculpture in sunset colors on a lawn beside the painted border fence." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A desert sunset, in the shape of a heart.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'eagle-heart.webp'} alt="A heart sculpture painted as an eagle with folded brown wings, on a lawn in front of a long mural on the fence." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The eagle heart, with the fence murals behind.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>A cantarito by the sea</h3>
  <p>
    By a quarter past twelve we had walked back down the malecón to a bar overlooking the beach, with an open terrace,
    a red-tiled roof and a view straight out over the water. The drink of the house was the <em>cantarito</em>, which
    comes from Jalisco: citrus juice, soda and usually tequila, served in a small clay jug whose rim is crusted with
    chili salt. This one came with a little extra drama: a stick of chili-covered candy laid across the top, and a
    clay jug painted with a cartoon woman and the word <em>La Tóxica</em>. On the counter behind it sat a row of
    pineapples, waiting their turn.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'cantarito.webp'} alt="A clay cantarito jug painted La Tóxica, with a chili-salt rim, on a wooden board at a seafront bar, next to a paper bag and pineapples." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A cantarito, with a view of the Pacific behind the bar.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2>Afternoon: Avenida Revolución</h2>
  <p>
    Then we went downtown. It is about six miles from Playas to the center of Tijuana, along the road that runs right
    beside the border. Downtown sits in the <strong>Zona Centro</strong>, a grid of streets just south of the busiest
    border crossing in the hemisphere, and its spine is <strong>Avenida Revolución</strong>, "La Revu". For a century
    this street has been where visitors from the north end up. In the 1920s, when Prohibition closed the bars of the
    United States, Hollywood drove down to Tijuana to drink, gamble and dine, and Revolución filled with bars, casinos
    and restaurants to serve them. Some of those buildings are still there.
  </p>

  <h3>Caesar's, and the salad</h3>
  <p>
    One of them is <strong>Hotel Caesar's</strong>, which has one of the better claims to fame of any building in Mexico.
    The Caesar salad is generally credited to Caesar Cardini, an Italian-born restaurateur who ran a restaurant in
    Tijuana during those Prohibition years. The story, as his family told it, is that on a busy holiday weekend in the
    1920s the kitchen was running low, and he improvised a salad from what was left: romaine, olive oil, egg,
    parmesan, garlic, lemon and croutons, tossed at the table for show. It was a hit with the Hollywood crowd, and
    within a few decades it was on menus all over the world. Caesar's, on Revolución, carries his name and keeps the
    tradition alive.
  </p>
  <p>
    That week, the entrance had been handed over to the Day of the Dead. Under the hotel's sign, beneath a fringed
    black parasol, stood a Catrina twice the height of a person: a skeleton lady in a bright pink gown with puffed
    sleeves and a mermaid train pooled on the tiles, roped off like a museum piece, with an ofrenda of framed photos and
    candles behind her. In the lobby, a glass table held a sugar skull the size of a beach ball, painted white with red
    and black flourishes, crowned with a spray of dried flowers in orange, red and gold.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'caesars-catrina.webp'} alt="A giant Catrina skeleton figure in a bright pink gown under a fringed parasol at the entrance of Hotel Caesars." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A Catrina at the door of Caesar's.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'caesars-skull.webp'} alt="A large painted sugar skull with a crown of dried flowers on a glass table in a tiled hotel lobby." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The sugar skull in the lobby.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The dining room is dark wood, white tablecloths, low lamps and framed old photographs, the look of a place that
    has decided the 1920s were its best decade and sees no reason to change. Next to our table stood the thing that
    makes Caesar's Caesar's: a battered wooden cart with a big wooden salad bowl on top, the kind they wheel to the table
    to toss the salad in front of you. Of course we ordered the Caesar salad; you can't come to the place it was
    invented and not. We started with a piña colada and something red and orange with a pineapple spear in it.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'caesars-cart.webp'} alt="Two cocktails on a white tablecloth, beside a wooden serving cart holding a large wooden salad bowl, in a dark wood-paneled dining room." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Drinks, and the salad cart.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The rest of lunch was a tour of the menu. There were tacos topped with pineapple salsa, avocado and a sprig of
    cilantro, with a little steel cup of salsa on the side, and grilled octopus, its tentacles charred and curled, on a
    smear of something pale and creamy under a tangle of greens.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'caesars-tacos.webp'} alt="Tacos topped with pineapple salsa, avocado and cilantro with a cup of salsa." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Tacos at Caesar's.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'caesars-octopus.webp'} alt="Grilled octopus tentacles with greens on a white plate." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Grilled octopus.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The main course was a round of puff pastry with a spiral pressed into the top and a sauceboat of dark gravy
    beside it, surrounded by roast potatoes, baby carrots and Brussels sprouts. We were at the table for a little
    under an hour, and walked out into the afternoon sun on Revolución.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'caesars-pastry.webp'} alt="A spiral-topped puff pastry with gravy, roast potatoes and vegetables." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Under the pastry.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>The street dresses for the dead</h3>
  <p>
    <strong>Día de Muertos</strong> falls on November 1st and 2nd. It is a time when families welcome back the
    spirits of the dead with <em>ofrendas</em>, home altars laid with photographs, candles, marigolds, sugar skulls
    and the departed's favorite food and drink, and visit cemeteries to clean and decorate the graves. It is far less
    somber than it sounds: the skeletons are dressed up, smiling and dancing, and the mood is closer to a family
    reunion than a funeral. On the last weekend of October, Revolución was getting ready.
  </p>
  <p>
    Out on the avenue at three o'clock, a lowrider had pulled up to the curb outside Caesar's, a purple pickup
    with flaming pinstripes and chrome wheels, its bed full of skeletons, a ghost, a cartoon zombie and a crown, all
    draped in fake cobwebs. The sidewalks were crowded with people out for the afternoon. A souvenir shop a block up
    had arranged its doorway like a stage set: a skeleton bride in a white veil holding purple flowers on one side, a
    skeleton groom in a top hat and tails on the other, and above them a row of painted portraits of Frida Kahlo, whose
    face, with flowers in her hair, is on souvenirs all over Mexico.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'lowrider.webp'} alt="A purple lowrider pickup on Avenida Revolución with skeletons, a ghost and cobwebs in its bed, in a busy street." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A lowrider, ready for the holiday.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'frida-shop.webp'} alt="A shop doorway with skeleton bride and groom figures under painted portraits of Frida Kahlo." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The bride, the groom and four Fridas.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Right next to it was the best surprise of the afternoon: a passage no wider than a hallway, its walls tiled from
    floor to ceiling in squares of <em>papel picado</em>, the cut tissue-paper banners of every Mexican fiesta, in
    pink, yellow, green, blue, orange and red. At the far end stood an ofrenda: tiers of skeleton figurines, candles
    and paper flowers, a red cloth, and more Fridas, one framed in orange flowers. A few doors on, outside a café,
    stood the second Catrina of the day, this one in a magenta gown with a wide pink hat and a foaming skirt of turquoise
    ruffles.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'alley-altar.webp'} alt="A narrow passage with walls covered in colorful papel picado leading to a Day of the Dead altar with skeleton figures and flowers." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>An ofrenda at the end of a paper-lined passage.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'pink-catrina.webp'} alt="A Catrina figure in a magenta gown, wide pink hat and turquoise ruffled skirt outside a café." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Catrina number two.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The Catrina herself has a surprisingly specific origin. She began around 1910 as a satirical etching by the
    printmaker José Guadalupe Posada: a skeleton in an elegant European hat, mocking Mexicans who aped French fashion
    and forgot that everyone ends up the same in the end. Decades later Diego Rivera painted her into a mural in a
    full gown, standing next to Posada and a young version of himself, and gave her the name <em>Catrina</em>, a
    word for a well-dressed lady. From there she became the face of the holiday, and in late October she seems to
    stand outside half the businesses in town.
  </p>

  <h3>The arch and the paper sky</h3>
  <p>
    At the north end of Revolución, where the avenue meets Calle Primera, rises the <strong>Arco
    Monumental</strong>, a soaring steel arch that has marked the entrance to downtown since the turn of the
    millennium. From its foot it climbs straight out of the traffic, glinting against the blue, with a screen hung
    from cables beneath it. A second TIJUANA sign stands at the corner, this one labeled <em>World Famous Revolución
    Av.</em>, the motto repeated along its base and a cheerful "#I love this place" tucked into the corner.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'arco-monumental.webp'} alt="The tall silver Arco Monumental over a street in downtown Tijuana against a deep blue sky, with palms and street lamps." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Arco Monumental.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'revolucion-sign.webp'} alt="A colorful TIJUANA sign reading World Famous Revolución Av. and Aquí empieza la patria on a street corner." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Sign number two, on Revolución.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Looking back south, the avenue runs downhill in a straight line under palms, with the art deco curve of an old
    hotel on the corner and a row of shopfronts in pink, green and orange. Nearby, a pedestrian street had been
    roofed with papel picado: hundreds of banners strung on lines from one side to the other, in red, white and green,
    the colors of the flag, cut into lace patterns of skulls and flowers. With the sun behind them they glowed like
    stained glass, throwing a dappled shadow over the crowd below.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'revolucion-street.webp'} alt="Avenida Revolución stretching away under palm trees and a deep blue sky, with old hotels and colorful shopfronts." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Avenida Revolución, looking south.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'papel-picado.webp'} alt="Hundreds of red, white and green papel picado banners strung across a crowded pedestrian street, backlit by the sun." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A roof of papel picado.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    That was about half past three. The camera roll goes quiet for the rest of the afternoon, and picks up again after
    dark with dinner at a Brazilian steakhouse across town. We spent a second night in Tijuana.
  </p>

  <h2>Day 2: Brunch and the line north</h2>
  <p>
    Sunday began late, with brunch at half past eleven in the <strong>Aviación</strong> neighborhood, a quiet
    residential area south of downtown, near the Agua Caliente racetrack and the country club. The camera roll records
    the table in detail, which is a fair indication of how the morning went. From there we made one more pass
    through downtown before turning north for the border.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-1030.webp'}><img src={P + 'daymap-1030.webp'} alt="Map of the route: Day 2: brunch in Aviación, a last look at downtown, and the line for the San Ysidro crossing." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Day 2: brunch, downtown, and the line for San Ysidro.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    The food was a long way from Caesar's dining room, and better looking than most brunches have any right to be.
    Pale green chiles were arranged in a star on a pool of dark, glossy sauce, with cucumber, red onion and a nest
    of carrot ribbons in the middle. A long grilled fish fillet, glazed and blackened at the edges, came with rice and
    a salad. Then came a wooden board of grilled octopus, cut into curls and charred at the edges, with potatoes
    and a dish of dark sauce, and, for dessert, a small red mousse covered in berry sauce and topped with blueberries
    and mint.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'brunch-chiles.webp'} alt="Pale green chiles arranged in a star on dark sauce with cucumber, red onion and shredded carrot." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Chiles in a dark, glossy sauce.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'brunch-fish.webp'} alt="A long grilled fish fillet with rice and a green salad on a white plate." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Grilled fish, rice and salad.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Of everything we ate that weekend, the octopus came up twice, on both days, in two very different kitchens: one
    on fine china under a tangle of greens, one sizzling on a board. Baja California has the Pacific on one side and
    the Sea of Cortez on the other, and seafood is never far from any menu here. The dessert arrived a good forty
    minutes after everything else, which suggests nobody was in a hurry.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'brunch-octopus.webp'} alt="Grilled octopus pieces with potatoes and a dish of sauce on a wooden board." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Octopus, round two.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'brunch-dessert.webp'} alt="A small red mousse dessert with blueberries and mint on a plate streaked with red sauce." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Dessert.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Just before one o'clock we were back in the Zona Centro. On a Sunday afternoon the streets were wide, sunny and
    quiet, the palms stock-still against the sky, a few people strolling along the sidewalk in the shade of the
    buildings. After the crowds under the papel picado the day before, it felt like a different city.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'downtown-sunday.webp'} alt="A wide, sunny downtown Tijuana street on Sunday, with palm trees, a traffic light and a few people walking." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Downtown, Sunday afternoon.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>The line</h3>
  <p>
    Then came the part of any trip to Tijuana that everyone warns you about: going home. The <strong>San Ysidro Port
    of Entry</strong> is the busiest land border crossing in the Western Hemisphere. Tens of thousands of people cross
    north every day, and on a Sunday afternoon, when half of Southern California is coming back from the weekend, the
    line of cars backs up through the city for blocks, lane after lane of them inching forward toward the inspection
    booths. Ours took hours. It was a long time to look at the back of the same red pickup, but, as it turned out, not
    a boring one.
  </p>
  <p>
    Fortunately, the line has its own scenery. Billboards stack up on both sides, in Spanish and English, aimed squarely at a
    captive audience: a barbecue festival thanking Tijuana for its success, lawyers, insurance for driving in the
    U.S., beauty clinics and, memorably, "post surgery therapy". And between the lanes, there is a market.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'queue-billboard.webp'} alt="View through a car windshield of the border queue, with a big festival billboard and vendor stalls ahead." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>In the line, with a barbecue festival saying thanks.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'queue-vendors.webp'} alt="Vendor stalls of crafts, hats and blankets beside the border queue, under billboards for clinics and lawyers." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Billboards above, a market below.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    It is not a figure of speech. On the medians and shoulders along the last stretch before the border, vendors set
    up full shops: racks of soccer and baseball jerseys (the San Diego Padres well represented), striped serapes,
    hammocks, sombreros and leather goods. And in front of all of it stood an army of painted plaster figures. There
    were bulldogs and chihuahuas, Sonic the Hedgehog, Pikachu, Hello Kitty, stormtroopers, Spider-Man, the Joker,
    the Scream mask and Jack Skellington, all lined up in rows and facing the traffic. Other vendors walked between the cars with
    trays of snacks, drinks and piñatas. You could, in principle, buy a whole living room's worth of decor without
    leaving the driver's seat. The pace of the line makes it a surprisingly reasonable plan.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'vendor-stall.webp'} alt="A vendor stall beside the border line crowded with painted plaster figurines of cartoon characters, dogs and superheroes, below racks of jerseys and blankets." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The plaster army of the San Ysidro line.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    And then, eventually, the booths, a few questions, a glance at the passports, and the freeway north into
    Southern California. The whole trip covered barely a day and a half and a few dozen miles. But it held a fence
    that walks into the sea, a beach where the art is louder than the steel, a salad with a hundred-year-old story,
    a street dressing for its dead, and a traffic jam you could furnish a house from. That is a lot for one weekend,
    and it is twenty miles from San Diego.
  </p>

